PRACTICAL NO: 2
TITLE: CONTROLLING RASPBERRY PI GPIO USING TELEGRAM BOT

AIM:
To interface Raspberry Pi 4 GPIO with a Telegram Bot using the Telegram Bot API and control an actuator/LED remotely over the internet.

COMPONENTS REQUIRED:
1. Raspberry Pi 4 with internet access (Wi-Fi or Ethernet)
2. 1x Light Emitting Diode (LED) or 5V Relay module
3. 1x 330 Ohm current-limiting resistor
4. Solderless Breadboard
5. Female-to-Male Jumper Wires
6. Smartphone or PC with Telegram application installed

CIRCUIT AND PIN CONNECTIONS:
LED Long leg (+) connected through 330 Ohm resistor to GPIO 17 (Physical pin 11)
LED Short leg (-) connected directly to Ground (Physical pin 6)

THEORY:

1. Telegram Bot API:
Telegram offers an open HTTP-based interface called Bot API. Bots are automated accounts managed by software rather than human users. They communicate over HTTPS and do not require telephone number registration.

2. Outbound Long Polling Mechanism:
Traditional remote access methods require static public IP addresses, router port forwarding, or Dynamic DNS (DDNS). The Telegram Bot API uses outbound Long Polling. The Raspberry Pi initiates an HTTPS GET request to the Telegram cloud server (api.telegram.org) and waits. When an end-user sends a command, Telegram delivers it in response to the pending request. This allows full remote control from anywhere in the world even behind strict NAT firewalls and mobile hotspots.

3. Authentication and Security:
Access to the bot is guarded by a unique secret cryptographic Bot Token generated by Telegram BotFather. In production environments, unauthorized access is prevented by checking the sender's Chat ID (msg['chat']['id']) against an authorized administrator whitelist before executing any GPIO switching command.

4. Actuation Process:
When the client enters /on, the Python script parses the incoming JSON payload, matches the string, and sets GPIO 17 to digital HIGH (3.3V). When /off is issued, GPIO 17 drops to digital LOW (0V). An acknowledgment text string is transmitted back to the user chat via bot.sendMessage().

TELEGRAM SETUP PROCEDURE:
1. Open the Telegram app and search for @BotFather.
2. Send /start followed by /newbot.
3. Enter a friendly name and a unique username ending with 'bot' (e.g., my_pi_gpio_bot).
4. Copy the generated HTTP API access token provided by BotFather.

CONSOLE SETUP COMMANDS:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3-pip python3-gpiozero
pip3 install telepot

PYTHON SOURCE CODE:
Save file as telegram_gpio.py

import time
import telepot
from telepot.loop import MessageLoop
from gpiozero import LED

led = LED(17)

# Replace with your token from BotFather
TOKEN = "YOUR_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN_HERE"

def handle_message(msg):
    chat_id = msg['chat']['id']
    command = msg['text'].strip().lower()

    print(f"Command received: {command} from Chat ID: {chat_id}")

    if command == '/start':
        reply = "Raspberry Pi GPIO Controller\nCommands:\n/on - Turn ON\n/off - Turn OFF\n/status - Check State"
        bot.sendMessage(chat_id, reply)

    elif command == '/on':
        led.on()
        bot.sendMessage(chat_id, "LED is now ON")

    elif command == '/off':
        led.off()
        bot.sendMessage(chat_id, "LED is now OFF")

    elif command == '/status':
        status = "ON" if led.is_lit else "OFF"
        bot.sendMessage(chat_id, f"LED Status: {status}")

    else:
        bot.sendMessage(chat_id, "Unknown command. Use /on, /off, or /status")

bot = telepot.Bot(TOKEN)
MessageLoop(bot, handle_message).run_as_thread()
print("Telegram Bot is running and waiting for commands...")

try:
    while True:
        time.sleep(10)
except KeyboardInterrupt:
    print("Stopping bot...")
finally:
    led.off()
    led.close()
    print("GPIO reset safely.")

